@meta
id: 26-13
title: A wall of JSON, made readable
chapter: 26
type: lesson
skills: filter-bang
par: 16
minutes: 5
vscode: paste it into a JSON beautifier website
open: webhook-event.json

@brief
One line, two hundred characters, copied out of a webhook log and saved
so somebody could work out why the capture was double-counted. Nobody can
read it in this state.

`jq .` reads JSON on standard input and prints it back formatted, two
spaces to a level. Filter the whole file through it.

This lesson needs `jq` installed. It is not part of coreutils, so if the
command fails with `not found`, that is why — install it, or use the
fallback in the solution, which is on every machine with Python.

@keys
| `:%!jq .` | reformat the whole file as JSON |
| `:%!jq -S .` | the same, with the keys of every object sorted |
| `:%!jq -c .` | the opposite — collapse it back to one line |
| `:%!python3 -m json.tool` | no jq on the box; four-space indent |

@hints
The whole file is one line, so the range is the whole file.
--
The command is `jq` and the program you are giving it is a single dot,
which means "this, unchanged" — the formatting is the side effect.
--
`:%!jq .` and Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :%!jq .<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Eleven keystrokes. Learning the `!` operator pays for itself the first
time you meet a minified anything.

`jq` is worth more than the pretty-printing. The dot is a program, and
the program can do any of the work:

    :%!jq .data              only the data object, everything else gone
    :%!jq -S .               every object's keys in alphabetical order
    :%!jq 'keys'             just the top-level key names
    :%!jq -c .               back to one line, for pasting into a test

`-S` is the one to remember for code review. Two JSON fixtures that are
supposed to be identical, written by two different people, diff against
each other uselessly because the key order differs. Run both through
`:%!jq -S .` and the diff collapses to the one field that actually
changed.

Without `jq`, `:%!python3 -m json.tool` does the formatting and
`--sort-keys` does `-S`. It indents four spaces rather than two, so pick
one and stay with it inside a repo. The same trick generalises: `:%!prettier
--parser typescript` formats the buffer if the project has prettier,
`:%!xmllint --format -` does XML, `:%!sqlformat` does SQL. Any formatter
that reads standard input and writes standard output is already a vim
command you did not have to configure.

One warning about pretty-printing with a filter: the command has to
parse. Feed `jq` a file with a trailing comma and it prints an error to
the screen, nothing to standard output, and takes your buffer with it.
That is the same lesson as the empty `grep`, and the same `u` fixes it.

@start webhook-event.json
{"id":"evt_8f21","type":"payment.captured","created":1774000000,"livemode":true,"data":{"orderId":"ord_1001","customerId":"cus_77","amount":4250,"currency":"GBP","refunded":false},"attempts":[1,2]}

@expected webhook-event.json
{
  "id": "evt_8f21",
  "type": "payment.captured",
  "created": 1774000000,
  "livemode": true,
  "data": {
    "orderId": "ord_1001",
    "customerId": "cus_77",
    "amount": 4250,
    "currency": "GBP",
    "refunded": false
  },
  "attempts": [
    1,
    2
  ]
}
